A||||8||falconhulk||Cooling down engine compartment||||1||
Z||000000||falconhulk||10-08-2001||01:03 PM||||I have an idea.  I want to mount 2 high cfm fans that I have on the grill vents.  I would like these fans to come on when I stop (hit the brakes) .  The other day it was 80 degrees and I was stuck in the traffic jam from hell.  The temp guage creeped up to 220 and I flipped on the heat so it did not go higher.  I would like these 2 fans to kick on in traffic but not when turning!  Anyone know which wire in that group near the battery is the brake switch?  The car has not overheated on me but hey I need a project and this seems cool.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||10-08-2001||01:06 PM||||Actually, that's completely normal.  The fan doesn't come on until 235*F, so you don't have anything to worry about.  If you want to reduce your engine temps, I suggest one of Rodney Dickman's low temp thermostats and fan switches.  I've got his 180* thermostat and 195* fan switch.  My car's temp never gets over 200 now.||||reg||1
Z||000002||falconhulk||10-08-2001||01:10 PM||||Where would I find "Rodney Dickman's low temp thermostats and fan switches"? My car rarely gets over 200 but I must confess I have no idea if its the stock thermo or not.  Actually I just have the parts laying around and want something to do with my car.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||10-08-2001||02:01 PM||||Why not just wire them to a rocker switch and flip 'em on in traffic?<BR>Gary||||reg||12
Z||000004||falconhulk||10-08-2001||02:29 PM||||heh heh cause that would be to easy and no challange at all.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||10-08-2001||02:42 PM||||Could try to hook them up to the relay that controls the radiator fan?  That way it would turn all three on when needed.  The relay is located ahead of the driver's side headlight on my '84.  You'd want to consult the Haynes manual for wiring diagrams.  84's had dual speed fans and were wired different.  The lower temp switch will help even more.  This is a project I've been pondering too, just haven't had the time to look into it closer.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||gomobile||10-08-2001||02:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBUZZ:<BR><B>Why not just wire them to a rocker switch and flip 'em on in traffic?<BR>Gary</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I use to have the same problem especially here in LA traffic. Got tired of it and did the Rocker Switch thing. Now anytime I am stuck in traffic I just switch the FAN on. Thus I don't have to worry about over heating. <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||Jefrysuko||10-08-2001||03:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>heh heh cause that would be to easy and no challange at all.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How about hooking them up to their own thermostat?  Take a look in Orge's cave.  He talks about doing this with the trunk mounted fan.  I always thought that would be cool.||||reg||1
